WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.154
- [Narrator] The idea of
robots taking human jobs

00:00:02.154 --> 00:00:06.494
might bring to mind a scene like this.

00:00:06.494 --> 00:00:08.010
But it's not hardware

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.530
that has white collar workers concerned.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.570
It's rapidly advancing software,

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.480
since artificial intelligence
is doing a lot more

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.250
than finishing emails.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.600
- Perhaps the biggest nightmare

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.470
is the looming new industrial revolution.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.750
- The erosion of the middle class.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.260
Already one of America's
most serious problems,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.960
could get much worse
with AI if we ignore it.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.502
- [Narrator] Here's how AI
is already taking over tasks

00:00:33.502 --> 00:00:36.946
at white collar jobs, and
why employees and companies

00:00:36.946 --> 00:00:41.913
are both excited and concerned
for the future of work.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.020
Generative AI can auto
complete your sentences,

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:46.959
write feature length films,

00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:49.125
or organize your calendar.

00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:51.560
And tools using it like ChatGPT

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.960
are taking on an increasing
role in white collar work.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.880
They can create first drafts of documents,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.240
presentations, images,
video, and product designs.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.570
Currently, 25% of work
tasks could be automated

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:07.650
by AI in the US according
to Goldman Sachs research.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.580
Generative AI helped make
this ad, and this one too.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.340
The company advertised here
said creating the image

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.180
for this AI generated ad
cost about 10 times less

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.890
than traditional methods.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.760
- This works much in the same
way as other kinds of AI,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.640
which are using statistical methods

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.210
on large amounts of data,

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.960
processing that through
computational power,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.900
and then generating out
a computer vision system

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.930
that says this image looks
a lot like these other cats

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.196
that I've been trained on.

00:01:38.196 --> 00:01:41.820
Generative AI is
functionally very similar.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.070
- [Narrator] It goes
beyond creating images.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.320
Alphabet is now using AI to run

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.630
anti-money laundering
surveillance programs

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:53.280
at banks like HSBC, and says
it's cutting out human error.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.600
- [Presenter] And when
the transactional data

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.824
and illicit behaviors change, as they do,

00:01:57.824 --> 00:02:01.020
so does the AML AI engine.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.280
- [Narrator] For some tech companies,

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.529
AI is contributing to job cuts.

00:02:04.529 --> 00:02:08.010
A May report said nearly
4,000 tech workers

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.900
lost their jobs to AI.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.760
Artificial Intelligence is also

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.653
eliminating future positions.

00:02:13.653 --> 00:02:15.870
Take IBM as an example.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.260
Its CEO said the company
could replace workers with AI.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.920
- I do believe that AI
is going to replace a lot

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.890
of what I'm calling white
collar clerical jobs.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.380
So the ones that are much more repetitive,

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.910
the ones that people do the same task

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.154
again and again and again,

00:02:31.154 --> 00:02:33.229
I think a good 30% of those roles

00:02:33.229 --> 00:02:35.910
could go away over five years.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.570
- Companies are making
evaluations about future hiring

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:44.010
on the basis of what they project
AI to be capable of doing.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.110
That's a much more tenuous area.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.850
- [Narrator] An IBM
spokesman said the company

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.640
was still hiring for
thousands of positions.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.730
- I think that what we've
seen in past deployments of AI

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.990
is that it's frequently used
as a justification for layoffs,

00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.148
but it doesn't necessarily mean

00:02:59.148 --> 00:03:01.938
that it is effectively replacing

00:03:01.938 --> 00:03:04.020
what human workers are capable of,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.920
or that it's increasing
workers' productivity.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.130
- [Narrator] Dropbox laid off
16% of its workforce in April,

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:12.900
but not to replace workers.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.380
Like IBM, it said it wanted
to invest more in AI,

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.000
which meant cutting elsewhere.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.170
- So we see demand growing for
these products a lot faster

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.400
than we could have anticipated,

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.038
and I think you could have
seen the technology coming

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:28.393
but just the way that the
interest went vertical

00:03:28.393 --> 00:03:31.080
is something that's way ahead of schedule

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.687
from our expectations.

00:03:32.687 --> 00:03:35.885
- [Narrator] At OpenAI, execs
say there may be more work.

00:03:35.885 --> 00:03:38.970
- I think that like the kind of sum total

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.660
of what is going to be needed
to make use of the technology

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.730
as a maximally productive technology,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.130
and kind of inflect
that productivity curve

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.380
up in the world will actually require

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.740
more man hours on top than you think.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.149
- But across industries,
workers and executives

00:03:55.149 --> 00:03:57.423
are cautiously looking ahead.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.060
AI's role in television
and film production

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.890
is a key point of negotiations

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.428
between the Writers Guild
of America and the studios.

00:04:08.428 --> 00:04:10.080
(protesters chanting)

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.420
Speaking to the "Wall Street
Journal" about the strike,

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.088
the co-showrunner for
the "Handmaid's Tale"

00:04:14.088 --> 00:04:17.490
laid out the writers union's concerns.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.833
- You know, we could tell
from the negotiations

00:04:20.833 --> 00:04:23.828
that the studios wanted, to had a plan,

00:04:23.828 --> 00:04:28.740
and we could deduce that
that plan was probably

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.620
one writer in a room
with one or two machines

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.750
creating an entire show.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.183
- [Narrator] Studios have
said they're committed

00:04:35.183 --> 00:04:38.490
to discussing the use of AI with the WGA.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:43.410
- The WGA strike I think is
a really important moment

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.353
to be paying attention to because
it's a place where workers

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:50.050
have taken the forefront in putting AI

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.511
at the front and center
of their negotiations

00:04:52.511 --> 00:04:54.480
about their working conditions.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.613
- If it's coming for us, the
creators of imaginary worlds,

00:04:58.613 --> 00:05:02.040
it is literally going
to come for everyone.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.080
- [Narrator] But there may
still be time to figure out

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.110
how to best integrate
AI into the workplace.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.340
- So I think it's really important

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.670
that in the present
moment, we preserve space

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:14.520
for a public conversation
around the deployment

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:16.622
of AI systems that ensures
that they're working

00:05:16.622 --> 00:05:19.702
in the public interest,
in workers' interest,

00:05:19.702 --> 00:05:23.225
and as well as in employers' interest.

00:05:23.225 --> 00:05:26.392
(upbeat bright music)

